  "issuedISO": "2026-11-27T08:09:00-10:00",
  "discussion": "No big updates are anticipated this morning, surf numbers look on track. An extra-large north-northwest (330 to 350 degree) swell continues to bring extra-large surf to north and west-facing shores today as it gradually decreases. Therefore, a High Surf Warning has been extended through this afternoon for north and west-facing shores of Niihau, Kauai, Oahu, Molokai, and north facing shores of Maui and the Big Island. The swell will fade further through the weekend, with surf falling to advisory levels by tonight and dropping below advisory level likely on Saturday. A small to moderate northwest swell arrives late Sunday and peaks on Monday, then fades through mid-week. A larger northwest swell follows this, arriving Wednesday and peaking in the latter half of next week. Surf heights along east-facing shores will continue to be elevated today due to wrapping from the north-northwest swell. As the swell fades, a medium-period northeast swell generated by a storm-force low to the northeast of the islands will keep surf elevated for east- facing shores today through Sunday. More typical, short-period trade wind swell will return by Monday. Surf along south-facing shores will remain tiny to small through the next few days, with a small bump possible this Saturday through Sunday.",
